The malware, dubbed "GoDoor," is a Go-compiled ELF backdoor with embedded `gob`-encoded C2 configuration. Static analysis begins with `strings -e l GoDoor | grep http` to extract embedded domains like `cdn-static[.]com`. Ghidra reveals embedded AES keys in `.rodata`, requiring a custom Ghidra plugin (`GhidraAESDecryptor`) for decryption.

Dynamic analysis uses Frida to intercept `crypto.Decrypt` calls:
```js
Interceptor.attach(Module.findExportByName(null, "crypto.Decrypt"), {
    onEnter: function(args) {
        console.log(hexdump(args[1], { length: 32 }));
    }
});
```
This reveals C2 endpoints like `hxxps://static.cdn[.]com/api/v1/status`. Uniquely, GoDoor uses QUIC (via `quic-go`) to evade detection, mimicking legitimate CDN traffic. Domain fronting is implemented via manual TLS SNI manipulation in the quic-go handler.

Mitigation involves killing the process via `killall -9 godoor`, blocking domains via `/etc/hosts`, and deploying eBPF-based runtime monitoring (`Falco`). Symantec reported GoDoor in APT-C-37 campaigns targeting energy sector Linux servers in Southeast Asia.

